act("Before the Official collects, the Storyteller reads out a rules note to Silke, in front of the table: a revolt can be called at any step, by anyone, the Warlord included, if two others agree on a cause from the list on the board card. Once it's called with two agreeing, play stops until it stands or is dropped. The Emperor may answer it, and it stands only if the caller and two others still back it. One called now, before the tax is paid, voids this year's tax. Under Chaos nobody pays tax, salaries or works, and nobody can buy new land; the harvest, the rent, the auctions and the Warlord carry on. The reserve is sealed and passes whole to whoever is acclaimed. The throne then goes only by acclamation: anyone but the deposed Emperor may claim it, a peasant or the Warlord included, and the claimants are counted on grain in hand, soldiers on the board and friends.", map_hl=[5])
act("Clark, Rich, Silke, Paddy and Rob are each asked, before the Official collects: pay the tax, or call a revolt and name the cause, or say they'd back one.", map_hl=[2,3,5,6,8])
say("Clark", "Thirty from me, Your Majesty, counted out and paid in full. The crown's collector pays first. I'll collect the rest cleanly, and I'll write it all down cleanly too: half the harvest in Year One, and the Warlord refusing outright while he camps on my fields. Five at year's end, I believe you said. I've written that down as well.",
 "Clark: One year at fifty isn't \"consistently excessive\" yet, so I pay and start the file. Rob's refusal and his lad under my Wall are the first two notes toward \"can't control the Warlord\", and if the Emperor does that again next year the table will see it in my handwriting.")
say("Rich", "Thirty, Your Majesty, gladly, and may the Summer Palace have a window facing north-west so you can see your most loyal province. Half is a brave number for Year One, but a brave crown is a strong crown, and I'm always for strength. Clark, count it twice. I'd hate for anyone to say the Gentry came up short.",
 "Rich: I pay, and I make a show of it, because a revolt in Year One hands the reserve to whoever shouts loudest, and right now that's probably the man with the soldiers. But half the harvest is steep, and I want everyone to remember I paid it smiling. Loyalty is cheapest to buy early and worth the most later.")
say("Silke", "Let's count it out loud. Thirty from me, thirty from Paddy, and the man sitting in the sea off our two coasts pays nothing and puts the kettle on. Half the harvest in Year One buys a Summer Palace I'll never see from the silk fields, while the crown \"doesn't knock on tents.\" If someone calls it on that cause, Your Majesty, I'm backing it. Cut the number to something a coast can live on, or show me the crown can make Rob pay, and I'll pay what I owe gladly.",
 "Silke: I'm not calling it myself, because the first one to stand up is the first one to get flattened. But Rob's already defied her and Paddy's paying the same thirty I am, so now Reina has to buy my thirty back with a lower tax before anyone else picks up the cause.")
say("Paddy", "I've got the thirty, Your Majesty, counted out right here, and I'm not one to owe anybody. But there's a boat sitting off my rice fields and the crown won't knock on its tent. So if someone calls it on the Warlord, I'm with Silke. Bring the number down to something a coast can stand, or tell me who keeps that boat off my shore, and I'll pay you before you finish asking.",
 "Paddy: I'm not calling anything myself. I'm only showing Reina there are two of us on the coast, so she has a reason to cut the tax before it comes to a revolt. If she gives us a fair answer, I'll pay the same day.")
say("Rob", "Well, I can't let the coast do all the brave talking. I call a revolt, and the cause is me: the Emperor can't control the Warlord. Reina, I'm sitting right here, I haven't paid a grain, and the crown \"doesn't knock on tents.\" Silke, Paddy, keep your thirty. And if you'd like the boat moved, we can talk about that once there's nobody on the throne telling us what to do.",
 "Rob: The best part is that I'm the cause and I'm the one calling it. If the two peasants hold firm, this year's tax is void, the reserve is sealed, nobody can build anything, and my soldiers keep marching while the rest of them fight over who gets acclaimed.")
revolt_called("Rob calls a revolt before the tax is collected, on the cause 'the Emperor can't control the Warlord'. Silke and Paddy have both said they'd back that cause, so two others agree, and play stops until the revolt stands or is dropped. Reina may answer it.")
say("Reina", "Rob, you want the throne to bend over a kettle, and it won't. The rate stands, because a crown that cuts on its first complaint is finished by its second. But Silke and Paddy, your grievance is the boat, not the number, and the crown hears you. The Winter Palace goes up this year instead of the Summer, so the Grand Canal reaches your coasts first. Ten grain each to you, from my own reserve, today, for a coast guard. And while I sit here, the crown pays half of every soldier you raise against him. Ask yourselves who keeps that boat away once there's nobody on the throne. Rob has already told you he'll \"talk about it then.\"",
 "Reina: I haven't cut a single point. I've turned the year's work toward their coast and bought their shields with my own grain, because a revolt needs three and I only have to take back one. Paddy said he'd pay before I finished asking, and he's the one I'm counting on to break first.")
act("Reina answers the revolt. The tax stays at 50%. This year's work changes from the Summer Palace to the Winter Palace (20 from the reserve), which opens the Grand Canal in Provinces 5 and 6, and the crown promises a coast canal section as its next work. The crown pays Silke and Paddy 10 each from the reserve now, as a coast-guard stipend, and promises to pay half of any soldier a coastal province buys against the Warlord for as long as Reina reigns.", deltas={pid(1):-20, pid(5):10, pid(6):10}, map_hl=[1,5,6], deal=True)
